    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [1, 3, 2, 3, 1]  ->  2   (3 > 2·1 at indexes 1,4 and 3,4)\nnums = [2, 4, 3, 5, 1]  ->  3   (4, 3 and 5 are each more than 2·1)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: count across sorted halves with two pointers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It's Count Inversions with a different condition. Split in half; pairs inside each half are counted recursively; pairs **across** the halves are counted while both halves are sorted. But the condition `a[i] > 2 · a[j]` doesn't match the merge's own comparison, so do a **separate counting pass** first: for each left value `a[i]` in increasing order, move `j` forward through the right half while `a[i] > 2 · a[j]`. Since `a[i]` only grows, `j` never moves back — the whole pass is linear. Then merge as usual."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: check every pair"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function reversePairs(nums) {\n  let count = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    for (let j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n      if (nums[i] > 2 * nums[j]) count++;\n    }\n  }\n  return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def reverse_pairs(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    return sum(1 for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n) if nums[i] > 2 * nums[j])"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int reversePairs(int[] nums) {\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n            if ((long) nums[i] > 2L * nums[j]) count++;   // 2 * nums[j] can overflow an int\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int reversePairs(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int count = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {\n        for (size_t j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {\n            if ((long long)nums[i] > 2LL * nums[j]) count++;   // 2 * nums[j] can overflow an int\n        }\n    }\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n²), space O(1).** Too slow for 5 × 10⁴ values."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Merge sort with a counting pass"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "reverse-pairs",
      "caption": "Before each merge, two pointers count the pairs across the sorted halves in one sweep."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function reversePairs(nums) {\n  const a = [...nums];\n  const sortCount = (lo, hi) => {                 // sorts a[lo..hi), returns the reverse pairs inside it\n    if (hi - lo <= 1) return 0;\n    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;\n    let count = sortCount(lo, mid) + sortCount(mid, hi);\n    let j = mid;\n    for (let i = lo; i < mid; i++) {              // both halves are sorted: j only moves forward\n      while (j < hi && a[i] > 2 * a[j]) j++;\n      count += j - mid;                           // a[mid..j) are all small enough for a[i]\n    }\n    const merged = [];\n    let p = lo, q = mid;\n    while (p < mid && q < hi) merged.push(a[p] <= a[q] ? a[p++] : a[q++]);\n    while (p < mid) merged.push(a[p++]);\n    while (q < hi) merged.push(a[q++]);\n    for (let k = 0; k < merged.length; k++) a[lo + k] = merged[k];\n    return count;\n  };\n  return sortCount(0, a.length);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def reverse_pairs(nums):\n    a = list(nums)\n\n    def sort_count(lo, hi):               # sorts a[lo:hi], returns the reverse pairs inside it\n        if hi - lo <= 1:\n            return 0\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        count = sort_count(lo, mid) + sort_count(mid, hi)\n        j = mid\n        for i in range(lo, mid):          # both halves are sorted: j only moves forward\n            while j < hi and a[i] > 2 * a[j]:\n                j += 1\n            count += j - mid              # a[mid:j] are all small enough for a[i]\n        a[lo:hi] = sorted(a[lo:hi])       # merge (sorted() on two sorted runs is linear in Timsort)\n        return count\n\n    return sort_count(0, len(a))"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] a;\n\nint reversePairs(int[] nums) {\n    a = nums.clone();\n    return sortCount(0, a.length);\n}\n\nint sortCount(int lo, int hi) {                   // sorts a[lo..hi), returns the reverse pairs inside it\n    if (hi - lo <= 1) return 0;\n    int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;\n    int count = sortCount(lo, mid) + sortCount(mid, hi);\n    int j = mid;\n    for (int i = lo; i < mid; i++) {              // both halves are sorted: j only moves forward\n        while (j < hi && (long) a[i] > 2L * a[j]) j++;\n        count += j - mid;                         // a[mid..j) are all small enough for a[i]\n    }\n    int[] merged = new int[hi - lo];\n    int p = lo, q = mid, k = 0;\n    while (p < mid && q < hi) merged[k++] = a[p] <= a[q] ? a[p++] : a[q++];\n    while (p < mid) merged[k++] = a[p++];\n    while (q < hi) merged[k++] = a[q++];\n    System.arraycopy(merged, 0, a, lo, merged.length);\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int sortCount(vector<int>& a, int lo, int hi) {   // sorts a[lo..hi), returns the reverse pairs inside it\n    if (hi - lo <= 1) return 0;\n    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;\n    int count = sortCount(a, lo, mid) + sortCount(a, mid, hi);\n    int j = mid;\n    for (int i = lo; i < mid; i++) {              // both halves are sorted: j only moves forward\n        while (j < hi && (long long)a[i] > 2LL * a[j]) j++;\n        count += j - mid;                         // a[mid..j) are all small enough for a[i]\n    }\n    inplace_merge(a.begin() + lo, a.begin() + mid, a.begin() + hi);   // the usual merge\n    return count;\n}\n\nint reversePairs(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> a = nums;\n    return sortCount(a, 0, a.size());\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n log n), space O(n).** Each level does one linear counting pass and one linear merge. In Java and C++, compute `2 * a[j]` in 64 bits — with values up to 2³¹ − 1 it overflows an int."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Brute force: check every pair",
          "time": "O(n²)",
          "timeWhy": "Every pair `i < j` is checked: about **n²/2**.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Just a counter."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Merge sort with a counting pass",
          "time": "O(n log n)",
          "timeWhy": "Each of the **log n** levels does one linear **counting pass** and one linear merge.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The **merge buffer**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A Fenwick tree (binary indexed tree) over the compressed values solves it in O(n log n) too — useful when the condition can't be counted with two pointers."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Negative numbers** — `[-5, -5]`: -5 > 2 · (-5) = -10, so that's 1 pair.",
        "**Overflow** — `[2147483647, 2147483647]`: 2 · 2147483647 overflows a 32-bit int.",
        "**Equal values** — `[1, 1]`: 1 > 2 is false, 0 pairs.",
        "**Counting inside the merge loop** — the merge's comparison is `a[p] <= a[q]`, not the pair condition; keep the passes separate."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Count Inversions** — the condition `a[i] > a[j]` fits inside the merge itself. Previous lesson.",
        "**Count of Range Sum** — merge sort on prefix sums, counting pairs whose difference falls in a range.",
        "**Count of Smaller Numbers After Self** — per-index counts through the same merge."
      ]
    }
  ],
